{
  "raw": {
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8938": {
      "title": "Professional Safety Officer",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Professional Safety Officer Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Professional Safety Officer Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603142 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Professional Safety Officer Position Type: Staff Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Non-Exempt Hiring Range: $22.36 per hour with excellent benefits. Location Tacoma, WA 98447. Department: Campus Safety & Information Posting Date: 10/06/2026 Closing Date When a sufficient number of qualified applicants have been identified. Work Schedule: Full-time, Thursday-Sunday 8:00pm-6:00am. Shift times and dates may be adjusted for training or department needs. This position is eligible to receive a pay differential of $1.00 per hour, for hours worked between 9:00pm and 4:59am. General Description: The Professional Safety Officer is responsible for the safety and security of the Pacific Lutheran University campus and its personnel. This is accomplished through providing a presence on campus, interacting positively and professionally with community members and guests, and performing a variety of daily tasks. Reports to the Assistant Director of Campus Safety Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Engage with community members in a culturally responsive manner and with the goal of developing on-going relationships. Proactively monitor activity on campus by providing presence on campus on foot or in a vehicle. Lock and unlock buildings and perimeter gates and check fire panels in buildings. Assist individuals in locating or obtaining resources to address issues or special needs. Respond to emergency incidents, ensuring proper procedures are followed and notifications are made. Administer first aid and operate an AED machine. Assist local fire and law enforcement personnel in the performance of their duties as requested. Provide assistance with vehicle jump starts, walking escorts, and shuttle transports. Use weaponless defensive tactics, employing verbal skills to diffuse volatile or hostile situations. Perform dispatcher duties including, answering multi-line phones, entering data into the computer system, and addressing walk-in visitors. Write reports on incidents. Operate university vehicles in conformance with local laws and PLU policies. Attend professional development training such as Security Officer Academy and ICS 100/200 and 700 certifications. Train new employees on department policies and procedures. Perform other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Excellent human relation skills; ability to deal with a wide variety of people; intercultural skill and capacity necessary to effectively engage individuals from a range of salient identities. Knowledge and skill in confrontation management. Ability to follow department policies, standard operating procedures, and directives. Ability to multi-task and make decisions quickly. Knowledge of radio operations, video console operations, and driving operations. Ability to deal with a variety of personalities in stressful situations. Ability to effectively and clearly read, write, speak and communicate the English language. Ability to communicate clearly on a handheld radio. Knowledge of basic computer skills such as MS Word, MS Excel, Google Mail, and Calendar, and use of the internet. Required Qualifications: 2 years of job-related safety/security experience, customer service experience, or equivalent training. High school diploma or GED . Valid driver’s license (Must have had a valid driver’s license for at least 2 years). First aid/ CPR / AED certification required and provided upon employment. Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background checks, including a driving abstract check, and pass PLU’s Driver’s Certification Training. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. Experience in an academic setting. Knowledge of PLU and its campus. Experience within a safety or security setting. Experience writing activity reports on a regular basis Work Conditions: Work is normally split between indoor office work and the field patrol environment. Exposure to all kinds of weather conditions and occasional exposure to physical risk. Physical Requirements: Ability to walk, run, or climb stairs for extended periods of time. Ability to sit and operate computer related equipment for extended periods of time. Special Instructions to Applicants: Other Information: PLU is a small, private university where caring means more than kindness and consideration — it means a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a diverse faculty, staff, and student body. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and minoritized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research or other work activities. PLU offers an excellent benefits package including tuition remission for employees and their dependents, generous retirement plan, medical, dental, paid vacation and sick leave, major holidays off (including Christmas/New Year’s break and extra time off during the summer), and many other great university benefits. PLU is a qualifying employer under the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program as a 501©(3) tax-exempt organization. Click here for detailed benefits information. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you 18 years of age or older? Yes No * Do you have a current valid driver's license which you have had for at least 2 years? Yes No * Do you have a High School Diploma or GED? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Resume List of Three References Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8929": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Musical Theatre",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Assistant Professor of Musical Theatre Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Assistant Professor of Musical Theatre Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit: Letter of application that specifically addresses your interest in, and qualifications for, the job responsibilities listed above. Curriculum vitae. Statement of teaching philosophy in which you describe your goals for student learning, the methods that you use to help students achieve them, and how you assess student learning. This statement should also explicitly address how you create an inclusive learning environment that is attuned to diversity and equity. A link to a digital portfolio demonstrating your creative work. This may include video clips of productions you have directed, music directed, or choreographed; and/or video of your own performance work. A link to a 15-20 minute video sample of your teaching (e.g., a class, workshop, or coaching) or a rehearsal you are leading. A sample syllabus for a course you envision teaching, such as “Musical Theatre Performance” or “Musical Theatre History”. The sample syllabus may be from a course previously taught or one proposed for a future offering. Full contact information for three references. For questions or more information, please contact Jeff Clapp ( clappja@plu.edu ) and/or James Brown ( brownja@plu.edu ), Search Committee co-chairs. Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603137 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Assistant Professor of Musical Theatre Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: $60,000-$70,000 per year + benefits package. Location Tacoma, WA 98447 Department: Theatre & Dance Posting Date: 09/25/2026 Closing Date Review of applications will begin November 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Work Schedule: Full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position available September 1, 2027. The academic year is from September to May. Classes begin on September 7, 2027. New Faculty Orientation will take place in late August. General Description: The School of Music, Theatre & Dance ( SMTD ) at Pacific Lutheran University invites applications for a tenure-track position, Assistant Professor of Musical Theatre, beginning September 1, 2027. The ideal candidate is an exceptional director, music director, and/or choreographer committed to musical theatre practice and pedagogy, who demonstrates artistic and educational leadership across the broader theatre discipline, will contribute to the cultural richness of the greater Seattle-Tacoma area, and foster an inclusive learning environment that attracts and sustains a diverse population of students. A commitment to excellence while working collaboratively with faculty colleagues and students across the university is expected. The PLU SMTD is a nationally recognized center for performance, education, and community. The Karen Hille Phillips Center for the Performing Arts, a state of the art facility that opened in 2013, provides a venue for most Opera, Theatre, and Dance productions, and a secondary space for some music performances. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Provide leadership, coordination, and development for the Musical Theatre program. PLU currently offers the Bachelor of Fine Arts ( BFA ) with concentrations in Musical Theatre, Acting/Directing, and Design/Technical. We also offer the Bachelor of Arts degree in Theatre, and a Minor in Dance. Direct and/or music direct one mainstage musical production annually. Teach coursework assigned in accordance with the qualifications of the applicant and the instructional needs of the SMTD . Load assignments may include: (1) Musical Theatre performance/scene study; (2) Musical Theatre history; (3) Musical Theatre audition techniques; (4) potentially private studio voice for musical theatre students; (5) other courses for the major (e.g., Acting or Directing) or pan-university courses based on expertise. Assume a position of leadership and high visibility in the musical theatre community in the state and region. Actively recruit top high school students for the musical theatre program and participate in SMTD recruiting activities (e.g., SMTD scholarship auditions). Advise and mentor students in the theatre program. Hold regular student hours. Participate in College of Professional Studies and SMTD faculty activities, as well as relevant service to the university. Other duties as assigned based upon the appointee’s strengths and department needs. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Demonstrated ability to work effectively with, teach, and mentor diverse groups and/or diverse students. Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion in the arts and in higher education. Knowledge of a diverse, broad musical theatre repertoire (both historical and contemporary) and current, equitable pedagogical and rehearsal techniques. Demonstrated ability in stage direction, music direction, acting/directing, and/or choreography for musical theatre. Required Qualifications: Terminal degree ( MFA , PhD, or DMA ) in Musical Theatre, Theatre, Music, or appropriate related area. ABD will be considered, but the degree must be completed by September 1, 2027. Significant professional experience (e.g., Broadway, national tours, LORT /major regional theatre) may be considered as a substitute for a terminal degree. Demonstrated experience directing or music directing musical theatre productions. Applicant must be legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications: Leadership experience in a professional or university theatre program. Demonstrated experience in teaching musical theatre at the university level. Ability to teach in a secondary area, such as acting (non-musical), directing, dance (jazz, tap), or specific vocal techniques (e.g., mix/belt). Piano/keyboard proficiency sufficient for teaching musical theatre performance courses, coaching, and rehearsals. Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants should submit: Letter of application that specifically addresses your interest in, and qualifications for, the job responsibilities listed above. Curriculum vitae. Statement of teaching philosophy in which you describe your goals for student learning, the methods that you use to help students achieve them, and how you assess student learning. This statement should also explicitly address how you create an inclusive learning environment that is attuned to diversity and equity. A link to a digital portfolio demonstrating your creative work. This may include video clips of productions you have directed, music directed, or choreographed; and/or video of your own performance work. A link to a 15-20 minute video sample of your teaching (e.g., a class, workshop, or coaching) or a rehearsal you are leading. A sample syllabus for a course you envision teaching, such as “Musical Theatre Performance” or “Musical Theatre History”. The sample syllabus may be from a course previously taught or one proposed for a future offering. Full contact information for three references. For questions or more information, please contact Jeff Clapp ( clappja@plu.edu ) and/or James Brown ( brownja@plu.edu ), Search Committee co-chairs. Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2025-26 academic year, 45 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 51.4 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,800 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Click here for detailed benefits information. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy List of Three References Sample Syllabi Links to Current Work List of Video Links Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8738": {
      "title": "Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Tacoma",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Tacoma Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Tacoma Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications are considered throughout the academic year and reviewed based on programmatic needs. If a review of your application indicates a match with our current needs, you will be contacted by phone or email for an interview. Applicants should submit a resume/cv by clicking on the “Apply” link above. Clearly indicate formal education and areas of practice expertise, as well as any specialized training and/or relevant certifications. For more information, please contact SarahAnn McFadden at mcfaddsm@plu.edu Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603036 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Tacoma Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Not Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: $3,400 per WTU for MSN; $4,000 per WTU for DNP. Location Tacoma, WA 98447 Department: Nursing, School of Posting Date: 04/29/2026 Closing Date Review of application materials will begin immediately and will remain open until filled. Work Schedule: Part-time; schedule is determined by the clinical placement need. Clinicals can be Monday-Tuesday-Wednesday-Thursday-Friday-Saturday or Sunday, 8, 10, or 12 hours shifts, and day, evening, or night shift depending on the clinical placement. General Description: PLU maintains a part-time nursing faculty pool in all areas of nursing specialties (Geriatrics, Medical/Surgical, Maternity, Mental Health, Pediatrics, and Simulation) for available assignments during the academic year. The PLU School of Nursing is dedicated to fostering a diverse and inclusive academic environment. We actively encourage applications from individuals whose backgrounds or interests align with this commitment. For over 70 years, the PLU School of Nursing has been preparing BSN graduates, and for more than two decades, it has educated graduate nurses. Our graduates are highly regarded throughout the Puget Sound region, demonstrated by a 100% employment rate within six months of graduation. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Clinical Instruction and Supervision Provide hands-on instruction and supervision to nursing students in clinical settings, ensuring the safe delivery of patient care and application of nursing theory to practice. Student Performance Evaluation Observe, assess, and provide timely, constructive feedback on students’ clinical skills, critical thinking, professionalism, assignments, and adherence to ethical and safety standards. Role Modeling and Mentorship Serve as a professional role model by demonstrating best practices in nursing care, communication, and teamwork; support students’ growth in confidence and competence. Provide coaching and mentoring for students outside of class time if they need additional support or instruction to be successful. Collaboration with Faculty and Clinical Staff Maintain open communication with ABSN program coordinator, course coordinators, full-time faculty, and healthcare facility staff to align clinical experiences with course outcomes. Documentation and Compliance Accurately document student attendance, clinical hours, and performance evaluations in accordance with program policies and regulatory requirements (e.g., BON standards, FERPA ). Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge of effective teaching and evaluation strategies. Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Effective verbal and written communication skills. Ability to be self-directed and solve problems effectively and efficiently. Strong organizational skills. Positive and professional demeanor. Required Qualifications: Graduate degree in nursing from an accredited college or university and from a nursing education program that is accredited by a nursing education accreditation body recognized by the United States Department of Education; OR a bachelor’s degree in nursing from an accredited college or university and from a nursing education program that is accredited by a nursing education accreditation body recognized by the United States Department of Education, and a graduate degree in a health or education related field from an accredited college or university. Appropriate experience/credentials relating to the subject matter in relation to the assignment. Legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background checks. Preferred Qualifications: Three years working in the area of specialty. Previous teaching experience in nursing. Specialty certification. Work Conditions: Conditions vary by course and include a variety of clinical practice settings. Physical Requirements: Depending in the assigned clinical teaching, may require prolonged standing and walking significant distances. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications are considered throughout the academic year and reviewed based on programmatic needs. If a review of your application indicates a match with our current needs, you will be contacted by phone or email for an interview. Applicants should submit a resume/cv by clicking on the “Apply” link above. Clearly indicate formal education and areas of practice expertise, as well as any specialized training and/or relevant certifications. For more information, please contact SarahAnn McFadden at mcfaddsm@plu.edu Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2024–25 academic year, 40.6 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 48.0 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,700 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae/Resume Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8942": {
      "title": "Svare-Toven Postdoctoral Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Svare-Toven Postdoctoral Teaching Fellow Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Svare-Toven Postdoctoral Teaching Fellow Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications must be submitted in English and are due by January 4, 2027. Applications must include the following: Proposal that describes your teaching philosophy and experience (if applicable), experience with a diverse student body, scholarly agenda, and potential contributions to and benefits from this fellowship (maximum 2000 words); Curriculum vitae; and Names and contact information for three professional references who can speak to your qualifications, past work, and potential as a postdoctoral teaching fellow at PLU. For questions or more information, please contact Dean Johnson at stephanie.johnson@plu.edu . Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603141 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Svare-Toven Postdoctoral Teaching Fellow Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: $48,000 plus benefits, housing in a campus apartment for the duration of the fellowship, and a $3000 moving allowance. Location Tacoma, WA 98447 Department: College of Liberal Studies Posting Date: 10/16/2026 Closing Date 01/04/2027 Work Schedule: Full-time fellowship available September 1, 2027 – May 31, 2028. The academic year is from September to May. Classes begin on September 7, 2027. New Faculty Orientation will take place in late August. Housing will be provided from August 15, 2027 – May 31, 2028. General Description: Svare-Toven Postdoctoral Teaching Fellowship: Since its founding in 1890 by Norwegian minister Bjug Harstad, Scandinavian immigrants and their descendants have had a distinct influence on Pacific Lutheran University. Today, these strong Scandinavian ties are most evident in the Scandinavian Cultural Center, whose mission is to enrich understanding of Nordic and Nordic-American cultures in conjunction with the Mortvedt Library’s Scandinavian Immigrant Experience Collection. The Svare-Toven Postdoctoral Teaching Fellowship at Pacific Lutheran University builds on this rich heritage to support the pedagogical development of recent PhDs who have expertise in Scandinavian Studies. As a one-year residential fellowship, it provides time to focus on the craft of teaching. One fellow is appointed each academic year (with possible renewal for a maximum of two years) to serve on the faculty at a rank equivalent to visiting assistant professor. Fellows are selected from applicants who hold a PhD or equivalent degree in Scandinavian Studies or in one of the following humanities or social science disciplines with a primary scholarly focus on Scandinavia: anthropology, economics, environmental studies, ethnomusicology, gender/sexuality studies, history, indigenous studies, languages, literature, philosophy, political science, religious studies/theology, and sociology. Fellows are in residence at PLU for the full academic year following acceptance (September 1, 2027 – May 31, 2028). Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Teach four undergraduate courses during the academic year: one in fall semester, one in J-Term, and two in spring semester, which may include one or two courses in the First-Year Experience Program and/or co-teaching an upper-division course. Participate in PLU’s mentorship program, including teaching mentorship. Present scholarly work as part of campus or community events, particularly in collaboration with the Scandinavian Cultural Center. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge of effective and inclusive teaching and learning strategies. Commitment to working effectively with a diverse faculty and student body. Demonstrated enthusiasm for teaching at the undergraduate level within a liberal arts setting. Ability to communicate effectively. Required Qualifications: Applicants must have received the PhD or equivalent terminal degree no earlier than December 2025, and the degree must be in hand no later than September 1, 2027. Hold a PhD or equivalent degree in Scandinavian Studies or in one of the following humanities or social science disciplines with a primary scholarly focus on Scandinavia: anthropology, economics, environmental studies, ethnomusicology, gender/sexuality studies, history, indigenous studies, languages, literature, philosophy, political science, religious studies/theology, and sociology. Applicants must be an emerging teacher-scholar who is not yet tenured or on the tenure track since earning their PhD. Applicants who do not yet hold the PhD, but expect to have it in hand by September 1 of the fellowship year must provide a letter from their home institution (department chair, head of graduate studies, or advisor) confirming the degree award schedule. Applicant must be legally eligible to work in the United States on their hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications: Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications must be submitted in English and are due by January 4, 2027. Applications must include the following: Proposal that describes your teaching philosophy and experience (if applicable), experience with a diverse student body, scholarly agenda, and potential contributions to and benefits from this fellowship (maximum 2000 words); Curriculum vitae; and Names and contact information for three professional references who can speak to your qualifications, past work, and potential as a postdoctoral teaching fellow at PLU . For questions or more information, please contact Dean Johnson at stephanie.johnson@plu.edu . Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2025-26 academic year, 45 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 51.4 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,800 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Click here for detailed benefits information. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Will you have a PhD or equivalent in hand by the date of appointment? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Fellowship Proposal (maximum 2000 words) Curriculum Vitae List of Three References Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8931": {
      "title": "Operations Manager, Accelerated Nursing Programs",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Operations Manager, Accelerated Nursing Programs Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Operations Manager, Accelerated Nursing Programs Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603132 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Operations Manager, Accelerated Nursing Programs Position Type: Staff Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Non-Exempt Hiring Range: $28.85-$32.21 per hour, plus excellent benefits. Location Tacoma, Auburn, and Lynnwood campuses, with approved remote work as operationally appropriate. Department: Nursing, School of Posting Date: 09/18/2026 Closing Date Until a sufficient number of qualified applicants has been identified. Work Schedule: Full-time; schedule may vary based on program and campus needs. Must be willing and able to regularly commute to Lynnwood, Auburn, and Tacoma campuses. In general, the position will require being on each campus at least one day per week. General Description: The Administrative Operations Manager for Accelerated Nursing Programs provides high-level administrative, operational, and project-management support to the Associate Dean and program directors responsible for the Pacific Lutheran University School of Nursing Accelerated Bachelor of Science in Nursing ( ABSN ) programs. This position manages complex administrative processes across multiple locations, coordinates work involving internal and external partners, develops and maintains systems that support efficient program operations, and exercises independent judgment in addressing non-routine matters. The position serves as a central point of coordination for the Auburn and Lynnwood ABSN programs and supports consistency, communication, compliance, and operational effectiveness across sites. Reports to the Associate Dean. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Manage and coordinate day-to-day administrative operations for the ABSN Associate Dean and program directors across multiple campuses. Serve as a primary administrative liaison among School of Nursing leadership, faculty, staff, students, university offices, clinical and community partners, vendors, and other external constituents. Coordinate complex calendars, meetings, travel, events, deadlines, and recurring program activities for the Associate Dean and program directors; independently resolve scheduling conflicts and anticipate operational needs. Develop, implement, and maintain operational systems, workflows, templates, tracking tools, and standard operating procedures that support consistent operations across ABSN locations. Manage high-priority projects and assignments on behalf of the Associate Dean and program directors, including establishing timelines, coordinating stakeholders, monitoring deliverables, and providing status updates. Prepare, edit, and distribute professional correspondence, reports, presentations, meeting materials, agendas, minutes, and other documents requiring accuracy, discretion, and knowledge of School of Nursing operations. Collect, organize, analyze, and summarize operational data for leadership decision-making, including enrollment, staffing, workload, scheduling, budget, student support, and program activity data. Escalate, manage, or resolve complex or sensitive requests from students, faculty, staff, and external partners; identify appropriate resources, elevate matters when needed, and ensure timely follow-through. Support preparation, monitoring, reconciliation, and tracking of program budgets, purchasing, invoices, contracts, purchasing-card transactions, reimbursements, and related financial documentation in coordination with university offices. Coordinate administrative aspects of faculty and staff recruitment, onboarding, appointment, contract, workload, evaluation, and records processes, while maintaining confidentiality and compliance with university procedures. Support regulatory, accreditation, and institutional reporting by organizing records, maintaining documentation systems, tracking deadlines, compiling evidence, and coordinating submissions as directed by program leadership. Coordinate logistics for orientations, faculty development, student events, pinning or recognition activities, advisory meetings, site visits, and other program functions. Manage and maintain shared electronic records, databases, calendars, contact lists, document repositories, and other administrative platforms; establish quality-control practices to ensure information is accurate, current, accessible, and secure. Represent ABSN administration on cross-functional committees, working groups, and special projects as assigned relevant to program delivery. Provide leadership, training, workflow coordination, and functional direction to student employees, temporary staff, or other administrative personnel when assigned. Identify opportunities to improve administrative efficiency, service quality, and business processes; research options and recommend solutions to School of Nursing leadership. Provide time-sensitive operational support during peak periods, absences, program launches, and urgent or time-sensitive situations as needed. Travel among PLU’s Tacoma, Auburn, and Lynnwood locations to support operational consistency as needed. Some remote work may be available based on operational needs and university policy. Perform other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging. Demonstrated ability to exercise initiative, independent judgment, discretion, and sound decision-making in complex and non-routine matters. Strong operational, project-management, organizational, and problem-solving skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities across several programs and locations. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including the ability to communicate professionally with executive leaders, faculty, staff, students, and external partners. Ability to anticipate needs, identify risks or barriers, develop solutions, and follow projects through to completion with limited supervision. Ability to synthesize operational information into actionable insights for leadership. Strong data-management and analytical skills, including the ability to create reports, monitor trends, reconcile information, and maintain accurate records. Ability to handle confidential, sensitive, and personnel-related information with discretion. Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office and Google Workspace, including spreadsheets, shared drives, calendars, forms, and collaborative documents. Ability to learn and effectively use university systems and specialized platforms such as Banner, Slate, NursingCAS, learning-management systems, scheduling tools, and financial systems. Ability to build collaborative relationships and work effectively in a fast-paced, team-oriented, multi-campus environment. Flexibility to adjust work location, schedule, and priorities in response to program needs. Required Qualifications: Bachelors degree and two years of progressively responsible administrative, executive support, program operations, project coordination, or office management experience OR 4 years of related experience. Education may substitute for some experience. High school diploma or GED . Will travel regularly among the Tacoma, Auburn, and Lynnwood locations. Reliable transportation. Finalist candidates must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background checks. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in business administration, higher education administration, organizational leadership, healthcare administration, or a related field. Experience in higher education, healthcare education, nursing education, or another regulated professional program. Experience supporting senior academic leaders, directors, or executives. Demonstrated experience coordinating complex projects, processes, or operations involving multiple stakeholders. Experience using independent judgment and managing confidential information. Experience with budget tracking, purchasing, contracts, personnel processes, event coordination, and administrative policy implementation. Experience working across multiple sites or with geographically distributed teams. Experience with Banner, Slate, NursingCAS, Google Workspace, Microsoft Office, and related enterprise systems. Work Conditions: Work is primarily performed in office and remote-work settings and requires frequent computer use, sitting, keyboarding, and virtual communication. Regular travel among the Tacoma, Auburn, and Lynnwood campuses is required. Occasional evening or early-morning work may be required for orientations, events, meetings, program launches, or urgent operational needs. Some remote work may be available in accordance with university policy and operational requirements. Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Other Information: PLU is a small, private university where caring means more than kindness and consideration — it means a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a diverse faculty, staff, and student body. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and minoritized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research or other work activities. PLU offers an excellent benefits package including tuition remission for employees and their dependents, generous retirement plan, medical, dental, paid vacation and sick leave, major holidays off (including Christmas/New Year’s break and extra time off during the summer), and many other great university benefits. PLU is a qualifying employer under the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program as a 501©(3) tax-exempt organization. Click here for detailed benefits information. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * This position requires regularly commuting to the Lynnwood, Auburn, and Tacoma campuses. Do you have reliable transportation? Yes No * Do you have a High School Diploma or GED? Yes No * Are you 18 years of age or older? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Resume List of Three References Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8915": {
      "title": "Director of Assessment, Information Management, and Educator Effectiveness",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Director of Assessment, Information Management, and Educator Effectiveness Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Director of Assessment, Information Management, and Educator Effectiveness Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603136 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Director of Assessment, Information Management, and Educator Effectiveness Position Type: Staff Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Non-Exempt Hiring Range: $33.65-$35.58 per hour, plus excellent benefits. Location Tacoma, WA 98447. Department: Education Posting Date: 09/16/2026 Closing Date When a sufficient number of qualified applicants have been identified. Work Schedule: Full-time, Monday-Friday, 8am-5pm General Description: Manages and supports School of Education (SoE) operations and student services. This includes reporting, educator preparation, and accountability policies and practices in collaboration with the Associate Dean of Education and Executive Director of Educational Leadership. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Assessment and Information Management Coordinate the accreditation and accountability requirements of the SoE Interface with OSPI Liaisons & PESB members on WAC and policy changes Coordinate program updates with Associate Dean and faculty Provide indicator data and other reports to PESB as required Complete and/or coordinate the following reports (and others as needed): Title II reports AACTE / CAEP reports State reports (e.g., Standard V updates, endorsement approvals) State data manuals School Annual Assessment Report Maintain on-going connection and leadership with WACTE membership and representation (e.g., attend annual meetings) Coordinate SoE partnerships, special projects, and grants School Operations In collaboration with Associate Dean and Executive Director of Educational Leadership: Organize the course schedule for all courses in the school (fall, J-Term, spring, and summer). Initiate and supervise the catalog changes for school programs Work with the Registrar’s Office to coordinate scheduling decisions, including classroom assignment. Revise program handbooks in coordination with the Associate Dean Support the Teacher Professional Educators Advisory Board ( PEAB ) and support with the administrative PEAB (i.e. note-taking, questionnaires, feedback) Student Affairs Provide testing support for students as appropriate Input student testing report into state databases as needed Coordinate with the Associate Dean, endowed scholarship and fellowship application and award process Educator Effectiveness Work with local school districts, schools, and university departments to develop, recruit and support programs, including diverse candidates Write and manage grants that support alternative pathways to teaching Provide student support and advising to those enrolled in the programs, particularly ARC and MAE Support program orientations SoE and Partnerships and Professional Development ( PPD ) Lead PLU Teach 253 initiatives Performs other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge of state teacher preparation standards and requirements of accreditation. Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Ability to use written and verbal communication with a variety of audiences, such as students, faculty, partners, and agencies such as the standards board. Ability to collect, analyze, and report data for a wide audience. Ability to organize and complete reports. Skill in problem-solving; ability to schedule. Ability to learn to use technology. (including the Microsoft Office Suite). Ability to support recruitment and retention of students. Ability to collaborate with faculty, staff, advisory board and outside agencies. Ability to drive to off-site in personal vehicle. Required Qualifications: Bachelors degree in Education, Curriculum and Instruction, or a closely related field. 3 years of job related experience. Graduate education may substitute for some experience. Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background checks. Preferred Qualifications: Experience as a teacher or teacher certification Experience working the standards board, accreditation, or in higher education Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Other Information: PLU is a small, private university where caring means more than kindness and consideration — it means a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a diverse faculty, staff, and student body. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and minoritized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research or other work activities. PLU offers an excellent benefits package including tuition remission for employees and their dependents, generous retirement plan, medical, dental, paid vacation and sick leave, major holidays off (including Christmas/New Year’s break and extra time off during the summer), and many other great university benefits. PLU is a qualifying employer under the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program as a 501©(3) tax-exempt organization. Click here for detailed benefits information. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a Bachelor's degree? Yes No * Are you 18 years of age or older? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter List of Three References Curriculum Vitae/Resume Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8936": {
      "title": "Budget &amp; Administrative Manager",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Budget &amp; Administrative Manager Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Budget & Administrative Manager Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603140 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Budget & Administrative Manager Position Type: Staff Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Non-Exempt Hiring Range: $20.75-$22.31 per hour, plus excellent benefits. Location Tacoma, WA 98447. Department: Nursing, School of Posting Date: 09/18/2026 Closing Date When a sufficient number of qualified applicants has been identified. Work Schedule: FT; Monday – Friday, 8am – 5pm. General Description: The Budget & Administrative Manager provides multifaceted support and coordination as a critical member of the School of Nursing staff. This position emphasizes key fiscal, contract personnel, and business process consultation for the School of Nursing and other units in the College of Health Professions (Social Work, Marriage and Family Therapy, and Kinesiology). The main area of responsibility includes the School of Nursing’s budgets and accounts. This position maintains a professional presence to all internal and external constituents and reports to the Dean of the School of Nursing. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Assist the SON Dean and collegiate leadership in the preparation of annual budget planning documents. Allocate budget amounts in consultation with the SON Dean. Reconcile monthly/annual budgets and accounts and prepare comprehensive budget reports. Implement and maintain processes for tracking expenses and identify/report budget variances to the SON Dean. Manage tracking for annual and routine expenditures, renewals, and income for SON operations. Collaborate with the Office of Sponsored Programs to ensure appropriate expenditures under grant and gift funds. Ensure accuracy for year-end close of assigned budgets and accounts and report on alignment with established budget goals. Provide consultation to CoHP staff on the development of internal fiscal and business operations practices to align with university policies. Process all part-time or contingent SON faculty contracts in collaboration with the Office of the Provost and HR. Payment Processing Draft scopes of work for independent contractor agreements on behalf of the SON and collaborate with Risk Services and Procurement to execute and finalize agreements. Draft and submit Requisition and Purchase Order requests on behalf of the SON . Collaborate with the Procurement Office in the development and execution of Requests for Proposals ( RFP ) and Request for Quotes ( RFQ ) for large SON purchases and services. Monitor and Manage SON’s use of Amazon Business workflow (and other suppliers as assigned), including but not limited to troubleshooting, monitoring and managing approval hierarchies, auditing invoices, and analyzing expenditures. Serve as the primary requestor on behalf of SON for PLU’s contract risk review process. Perform other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Financial knowledge of accounting practices. Strong analytic and data management skills. Proficiency in use of MS Excel (specifically creating advanced formulas, budget documents, and databases), PowerPoint, and MS Word. Strong organizational and time management skills. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Awareness of legal and regulatory environments. Ability to manage multiple, complex activities simultaneously. Excellent problem-solving skills and judgment. Excellent understanding of confidentiality, use of discretion in communications, reporting lines, and policy/procedure. Team-based collaborative approach. Ability to independently and proactively learn new technology applications for electronic data management and other applications. Ability to ensure timeliness and proactively anticipate and manage all timeline requirements. Integrity in all areas of responsibility. Ability to maintain open, welcoming, and responsive communications in all interactions. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree or 2 years of related experience. Education may substitute for some experience. High school diploma or GED . Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background checks. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree with business/administrative focus. Experience in an academic system. Experience with professional degree programs and regulatory requirements. Work Conditions: The job is largely performed in an office setting. Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Other Information: PLU is a small, private university where caring means more than kindness and consideration — it means a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a diverse faculty, staff, and student body. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and minoritized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research or other work activities. PLU offers an excellent benefits package including tuition remission for employees and their dependents, retirement plan, medical, dental, paid vacation and sick leave, major holidays off (including Christmas/New Year’s break), additional flexible time off, and many other great university benefits. PLU is a qualifying employer under the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program as a 501©(3) tax-exempt organization. Click here for detailed benefits information. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you 18 years of age or older? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Do you have a High School Diploma or GED? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Resume Optional Documents List of Three References Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8927": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Marriage and Family Therapy",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Assistant/Associate Professor of Marriage and Family Therapy Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Assistant/Associate Professor of Marriage and Family Therapy Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit the following: Letter of Interest. The letter should include your teaching philosophy, scholarly interests, commitment to systemic thinking, and how you create a learning environment that is attuned to inclusion and equity. Curriculum vitae. List of three references. Teaching evaluations will be requested from semi-finalists. For questions or more information, please contact David Ward at warddb@plu.edu Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603134 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Assistant/Associate Professor of Marriage and Family Therapy Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: Salary commensurate with experience and rank. $93,333-$102,667 per year (12 mo base) + benefits package if hired at the rank of Assistant Professor; $102,667-$116,000 per year (12 mo base) + benefits package if hired at the rank of Associate Professor. Location Tacoma, Washington 98447 Department: Marriage & Family Therapy Dept Posting Date: 09/25/2026 Closing Date Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Work Schedule: Full time, Assistant/Associate Professor position available June 1, 2027. 12-month position (June-May). Faculty load equal to 8 courses with a mixture of teaching and program administration. Position is in-person. General Description: The Department of Marriage and Family Therapy at Pacific Lutheran University seeks an Assistant/Associate Professor to begin June 1, 2027 or September 1, 2027. The position may be tenured/tenure-track (hiring at the level of Assistant Professor or Associate Professor based upon qualifications). The position includes a combination of teaching, supervising, and administrative responsibilities in a COAMFTE -accredited program equivalent to eight four-credit courses. The MFT Program at Pacific Lutheran University is accredited by the Commission on Accreditation for Marriage and Family Therapy Education of the AAMFT ( COAMFTE ). The program consists of four full- time faculty, who teach a diverse student body of approximately 45 students, and provide on-site clinical supervision to second-year students. The program focuses on in-depth training in systemic clinical skills, utilizing the benefits of small student cohorts, strong faculty-student relationships, and an on-site clinic with live faculty supervision. Additionally, the program partners with program clinical supervisors at various community mental health agencies where students complete their practica training and expand their clinical experience. The program’s commitment to inclusion and equity is infused throughout the curriculum. Further information about the program can be found on the website at https://www.plu.edu/mft/ Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Teach couple and family therapy graduate courses with an emphasis on inclusive teaching practices. Provide clinical supervision of MFT graduate students. Assist in administrative responsibilities of the program, which may include overseeing practicum, internship site coordination, clinical training & development, and internship placements. Advise graduate students. Participate in the larger life of the university through governance, committees and other service activities. Develop and advance a program of scholarship. Hold regular student hours. Attend regular department meetings. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: We seek faculty who view themselves foremost as educators, possess a commitment to effective and inclusive teaching strategies, and are open to feedback and growth. Our students come from diverse backgrounds with different values and beliefs, but unite in a commitment to systemic, contextual, and equitable care for clients. Many are first-generation college students, non-traditional students, students of color, and LGBTQ+ students. We especially encourage applications from individuals who can hold space for conversations across differences and who share our commitment to diversity, inclusion, and equity in their teaching, research, and service. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Marriage and Family Therapy from a COAMFTE -accredited program or its equivalent. ABD will be considered, but Ph.D. must be in hand by the start date of the position. Evidence of strong teaching and supervisory skills, and a demonstrated ability to work effectively with students and colleagues. Commitment to using inclusive and anti-racist pedagogy. Clinical or research emphasis on marginalized populations. American Association of Marriage and Family Therapy ( AAMFT ) Clinical Member and Approved Supervisor or Approved Supervisor Candidate. Applicant must be legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications: Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants should submit the following: Letter of Interest. The letter should include your teaching philosophy, scholarly interests, commitment to systemic thinking, and how you create a learning environment that is attuned to inclusion and equity. Curriculum vitae. List of three references. Teaching evaluations will be requested from semi-finalists. For questions or more information, please contact David Ward at warddb@plu.edu Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2025-26 academic year, 45 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 51.4 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,800 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Click here for detailed benefits information. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No * Will you have a PhD or equivalent in hand by the date of appointment? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae List of Three References Optional Documents Teaching Evaluations Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8940": {
      "title": "General Maintenance Technician II",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | General Maintenance Technician II Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home General Maintenance Technician II Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603148 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: General Maintenance Technician II Position Type: Staff Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Non-Exempt Hiring Range: $25.00-$27.00 per hour, plus excellent benefits. Location Tacoma, WA 98447 Department: Facilities Management Posting Date: 10/09/2026 Closing Date When a sufficient number of candidates have been identified Work Schedule: Full-time; Monday – Friday; 12:00 pm to 8:30 pm General Description: The General Maintenance Technician II will perform a variety of maintenance duties to include electrical, plumbing, mechanical, boilers, roofing and gutter repairs, lighting indoor/outdoor, window and blind repair, fire extinguishers, fire risers, lock hardware, and minor carpentry to meet general maintenance needs on campus. Reports to the Director of Operations & Maintenance / Shift Repair Lead. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Perform minor electrical and plumbing and repairs. Make minor roof and gutter repairs on all campus facilities. Secure damaged windows and coordinate replacement with outside contractors. Replace building lights including lamps and ballasts as needed. Assist Electrician with indoor/outdoor lighting repairs. Assist Plumber with water filter replacements and battery changes as needed. Assist Building Engineer with boiler checks. Make minor repair to door hardware and replace lock cores, under Locksmith’s guidance. Provide back-up in other areas as needed. Perform building fire extinguisher checks and schedule annual inspections. Escort all building equipment inspectors through buildings during annual inspections (e.g., elevator, boilers, sprinkler, fire department, insurance representatives). Evaluate and/or perform emergency repairs of all types as needed during night or weekend shifts. Assist securing buildings having door/lock problems, open doors for contractors, and open/lock gates Assist with monitoring and maintaining chemical balance in the swimming pool. Perform other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Interpersonal and communication skills (verbally and written). Technical/software skills. Initiative. Teamwork. Ability to use equipment. Decision-making. Problem-solving. Must work well with coworkers and customers. Ability to make decisions and work independently without close supervision is essential. Must be mechanically inclined and able to perform emergency repairs in many areas. Knowledge of computer operation and technology is highly desirable. Work should be well organized and able to prioritize work. Must be able to remain calm during stressful periods. Proficiency using a multimeter for testing voltage and amperage. Ability to use scaffolding, ladders, safety harnesses, and high lift operation (training provided). Work should be well organized and able to prioritize work . Required Qualifications: High school diploma or GED . One year of experience in building maintenance or similar craft. Education including technical school or apprenticeship can substitute for experience. WA State Class IV Boiler Fireman certification or the ability to acquire within 18 months of employment (Training will be provided for selected candidates, if not already acquired). Must be mechanically inclined and able to perform emergency repairs in many areas. Work shifts, days, On-Call, requirements may vary. Valid Driver’s License (Must have had a valid driver’s license for at least 2yrs). Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background check, declaration regarding sexual misconduct, pass PLU’s Driver’s Certification Training, and provide an original copy of their Driving Record (5-year Employment Abstract). Preferred Qualifications: Current Boiler License. Knowledge of computer operation and technology. Work Conditions: Work conditions vary considerably; employee may often be exposed to hot, dry, and dusty conditions, or sometimes to cold, wet, and/or humid conditions. Work is often around very heavy equipment with large or small awkward tools and spaces. Employee may occasionally work in high places. Employee may be exposed to hazardous conditions; i.e., fire boxes, relief valves, hot pipes, gas lines, hazardous chemicals. Noise level is sometimes high. Some overtime is required, and vacation approval may be limited during peak activity periods. Employee must adhere to all safety standards and work practices established by Facilities Management. Work shifts and days will vary. Some weekend swing shift work is required. Physical Requirements: The employee is regularly required to stand and walk, and may occasionally be required to climb, balance, or work in cramped areas. The employee must be able to bend, reach, stoop, and kneel while working in a variety of areas. Requires good physical strength. The employee must be able to regularly lift and/or move up to 10 pounds, frequently lift or move up to 25 pounds, and occasionally lift or move up to 50 pounds. Good hearing and vision are required. Special Instructions to Applicants: Other Information: PLU is a small, private university where caring means more than kindness and consideration — it means a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a diverse faculty, staff, and student body. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and minoritized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research or other work activities. PLU offers an excellent benefits package including tuition remission for employees and their dependents, generous retirement plan, medical, dental, paid vacation and sick leave, major holidays off (including Christmas/New Year’s break and extra time off during the summer), and many other great university benefits. PLU is a qualifying employer under the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program as a 501©(3) tax-exempt organization. Click here for detailed benefits information. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a High School Diploma or GED? Yes No * Do you have a current valid driver's license which you have had for at least 2 years? Yes No * Are you 18 years of age or older? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Optional Documents Cover Letter List of Three References Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8930": {
      "title": "Associate Dean, School of Music, Theatre &amp; Dance, Associate or Full Professor",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Associate Dean, School of Music, Theatre &amp; Dance, Associate or Full Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Associate Dean, School of Music, Theatre & Dance, Associate or Full Professor Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit: Letter of application that specifically addresses your interest in, and qualifications for, the job responsibilities listed above. Curriculum Vitae. Statement describing leadership vision, particularly for a School of Music, Theatre & Dance housed within a comprehensive liberal arts university. Names, email addresses and telephone numbers of five references. Additional materials may be required at a later date. Nominations are also accepted. For nominations, questions, or more information, please contact Cameron Bennett, Dean, College of Professional Studies, at cbennett@plu.edu . Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603138 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Associate Dean, School of Music, Theatre & Dance, Associate or Full Professor Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: $96,000 to $122,667 per year (12 month base) + benefits package. Location Tacoma, WA 98447 Department: Music Department Posting Date: 09/25/2026 Closing Date Review of application materials will begin October 26, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Work Schedule: Full time, Associate Dean position available July 1, 2027. 12-month contract (June-May); 67% administrative release and 33% teaching. General Description: The School of Music, Theatre & Dance ( SMTD ) of the College of Professional Studies at Pacific Lutheran University in Tacoma, WA, seeks a dynamic associate dean to lead the unit with a vision to sustain and advance a widely acclaimed program of artistic and academic excellence. Rank of Associate or Full Professor. Teaching responsibilities based upon expertise and needs. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Provide academic and artistic leadership of a vibrant and multifaceted School of Music, Theatre & Dance—overseeing all policy, personnel, and budgetary matters. Sustain and advance the SMTD through active student recruitment and faculty development. Foster a collaborative leadership environment and be responsible for: academic planning; assessment and enhancement of the program and curriculum; faculty evaluation; coordination of teaching assignments and schedules; facilities use; budgeting and allocation of resources; student advisement; annual scholarship process; oversight of a robust international and national ensemble touring schedule; and accreditation. Work collegially within a university-wide faculty governance structure. Foster support for College-wide and university goals. Advocate effectively for the SMTD within and outside the university—regionally and the national music community. Encourage collaborative interdisciplinary programming—within the School, College, and across the university. Teach a 33% load (equivalent of two courses) based upon expertise and the needs of the school. Supervise office personnel. Work closely with the Dean of the College of Professional Studies to develop additional resources for the SMTD . Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Demonstrated excellence as a teacher/scholar/artist with a specialty in a field taught within the school. Demonstrated excellence as an administrator with a distinguished record of continuing research/creative activity. Superior interpersonal and communication skills. Knowledge of emerging technologies for use in the classroom and in research in order to manage and sustain school initiatives. Required Qualifications: Terminal degree in music, theatre or dance (Ph.D, DMA , or MFA ) and a record of excellence in teaching and artistic or scholarly activity. Academic leadership experience at the level of chair, associate dean, or dean and/or a demonstrable track record of increasing leadership responsibilities. An understanding of all disciplines housed in the school is essential. Applicant must be legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications: At least three years of demonstrable administrative experience including a history of resource management, recruiting, promotion of university music, theatre or dance programs, program development, community outreach, strategic planning, experience with national accreditation agencies, and success in fundraising. Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants should submit: Letter of application that specifically addresses your interest in, and qualifications for, the job responsibilities listed above. Curriculum Vitae. Statement describing leadership vision, particularly for a School of Music, Theatre & Dance housed within a comprehensive liberal arts university. Names, email addresses and telephone numbers of five references. Additional materials may be required at a later date. Nominations are also accepted. For nominations, questions, or more information, please contact Cameron Bennett, Dean, College of Professional Studies, at cbennett@plu.edu . Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2025-26 academic year, 45 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 51.4 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,800 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Click here for detailed benefits information. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Will you have a terminal degree in hand by the date of appointment? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Statement List of Five References Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8739": {
      "title": "Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Lynnwood",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Lynnwood Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Lynnwood Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications are considered throughout the academic year and reviewed based on programmatic needs. If a review of your application indicates a match with our current needs, you will be contacted by phone or email for an interview. Applicants should submit a resume/cv by clicking on the “Apply” link above. Clearly indicate formal education and areas of practice expertise, as well as any specialized training and/or relevant certifications. For more information, please contact Tiffany Zyniewicz at (425) 440-2984 or tiffany.zyniewicz@plu.edu Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603039 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Lynnwood Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Not Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: $3,400 per WTU for MSN; $4,000 per WTU for DNP. Location Lynnwood, WA 98036 Department: Nursing, School of Posting Date: 04/29/2026 Closing Date Review of application materials will begin immediately and will remain open until filled. Work Schedule: Part-time; schedule is determined by the clinical placement need. Clinicals can be Monday-Tuesday-Wednesday-Thursday-Friday-Saturday or Sunday, 8, 10, or 12 hours shifts, and day, evening, or night shift depending on the clinical placement. General Description: PLU maintains a part-time nursing faculty pool in all areas of nursing specialties (Geriatrics, Medical/Surgical, Maternity, Mental Health, Pediatrics, and Simulation) for available assignments during the academic year. The PLU School of Nursing is dedicated to fostering a diverse and inclusive academic environment. We actively encourage applications from individuals whose backgrounds or interests align with this commitment. For over 70 years, the PLU School of Nursing has been preparing BSN graduates, and for more than two decades, it has educated graduate nurses. Our graduates are highly regarded throughout the Puget Sound region, demonstrated by a 100% employment rate within six months of graduation. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Clinical Instruction and Supervision Provide hands-on instruction and supervision to nursing students in clinical settings, ensuring the safe delivery of patient care and application of nursing theory to practice. Student Performance Evaluation Observe, assess, and provide timely, constructive feedback on students’ clinical skills, critical thinking, professionalism, assignments, and adherence to ethical and safety standards. Role Modeling and Mentorship Serve as a professional role model by demonstrating best practices in nursing care, communication, and teamwork; support students’ growth in confidence and competence. Provide coaching and mentoring for students outside of class time if they need additional support or instruction to be successful. Collaboration with Faculty and Clinical Staff Maintain open communication with ABSN program coordinator, course coordinators, full-time faculty, and healthcare facility staff to align clinical experiences with course outcomes. Documentation and Compliance Accurately document student attendance, clinical hours, and performance evaluations in accordance with program policies and regulatory requirements (e.g., BON standards, FERPA ). Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge of effective teaching and evaluation strategies. Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Effective verbal and written communication skills. Ability to be self-directed and solve problems effectively and efficiently. Strong organizational skills. Positive and professional demeanor. Required Qualifications: Graduate degree in nursing from an accredited college or university and from a nursing education program that is accredited by a nursing education accreditation body recognized by the United States Department of Education; OR a bachelor’s degree in nursing from an accredited college or university and from a nursing education program that is accredited by a nursing education accreditation body recognized by the United States Department of Education, and a graduate degree in a health or education related field from an accredited college or university. Appropriate experience/credentials relating to the subject matter in relation to the assignment. Legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background checks. Preferred Qualifications: Three years working in the area of specialty. Previous teaching experience in nursing. Specialty certification. Work Conditions: Conditions vary by course and include a variety of clinical practice settings. Physical Requirements: Depending in the assigned clinical teaching, may require prolonged standing and walking significant distances. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications are considered throughout the academic year and reviewed based on programmatic needs. If a review of your application indicates a match with our current needs, you will be contacted by phone or email for an interview. Applicants should submit a resume/cv by clicking on the “Apply” link above. Clearly indicate formal education and areas of practice expertise, as well as any specialized training and/or relevant certifications. For more information, please contact Tiffany Zyniewicz at (425) 440-2984 or tiffany.zyniewicz@plu.edu Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2024–25 academic year, 40.6 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 48.0 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,700 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae/Resume Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8943": {
      "title": "Lead Cook",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Lead Cook Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Lead Cook Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603150 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Lead Cook Position Type: Staff Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Non-Exempt Hiring Range: $23.68 per hour, plus excellent benefits. Location Tacoma, WA 98447. Department: Hospitality Services & Campus Restaurants Administration Posting Date: 10/16/2026 Closing Date When a sufficient number of qualified applicants have been identified. Work Schedule: Full-time; days and shifts vary based on catering schedule, the schedule below represents a non catering week Catering PM Float Schedule: Saturday – Off Sunday – Thursday – 10 am – 7:30 pm Friday – Off HSCR Employees are considered Essential University Employees and as such schedule days/times may vary based on University needs. General Description: This is a great opportunity in a busy kitchen with top-notch benefits; uniform provided, retirement plan, tuition remission for you and dependents, medical and dental, use of the University facilities as well as vacation, sick and holiday pay. On average, the university has 14 paid holidays per year. We are always closed (with holiday pay) over the Thanksgiving and Christmas holidays. Be ready to work with a great team in a high-volume kitchen serving breakfast, lunch, and dinner. Dining and Culinary Services is independently operated and provides the nutritional foundation for the academic and co-curricular success of Pacific Lutheran University students. The Lead Cook prepares a variety of foods for the main dining facility, The Commons, and leads a team of line cooks, prep cooks and college students with strong emphasis on proper cooking techniques, safety, sanitation, sustainability, and exemplary customer service. This position reports to the Executive Chef. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Monitor and supervise student and temporary workers in customer service, safety and sanitation. Prepare scratch menu items for the main university dining facility. Comply with all PLU and Health Department safety standards. Conform to high standards of quality and sanitation; follow proper cleaning, maintenance and reporting procedures. Read menu to estimate food requirements in advance. Monitor and adjust thermostat controls to regulate temperature of ovens, combi-ovens, fryers, boilers, grills, range top, induction burners, and steam kettles, to ensure food quality. Measure and mix ingredients according to recipes, using a variety or kitchen utensils and equipment, such as blenders, mixers, grinders, slicers, and tenderizers, to prepare soups, salads, pasta, pizza, desserts, sauces, and casseroles. Sauté, grill, bake, and fry a la minute or batch cooked orders for line in a high energy, fast paced environment. Bake, roast, broil, and steam meats, fish, vegetables, and other foods. Season food during mixing or cooking to department standards. Carve meat, portion food on serving plates, add gravies, sauces, and garnishes to fill orders. Properly store, rotate, and label product. Monitor condition of the kitchen and equipment it contains; follows proper cleaning, maintenance and reporting procedures. Wash, peel, cut, and shred vegetables and fruits to prepare them for use. Butcher chicken, fish, and shellfish. Maintain correct department signage and pricing, and answer customer questions. Perform other duties as assigned. Campus Restaurants team members are essential university personnel. In the event of PLU declaring emergency status essential university personnel must contact supervisor to obtain schedule. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Excellent interpersonal skills and ability to communicate effectively with other university leadership, team members, and customers. Ability to work independently and as part of a team. Ability to multitask and utilize effective time management during production. Ability to measure, cut, or otherwise work on materials or objects with great precision. Ability to detect food and labor waste issues and develop a plan of corrective action. Ability to provide exemplary customer service constantly and consistently. Knowledge of vegetarian and vegan cuisine, gluten-free and allergy diets. Knowledge of and ability to use commercial kitchen equipment, including the proper use of slicers, knives, box cutters, a dumpster, and all related equipment. Ability to work flexible schedule including weekends, evenings, and holidays. Required Qualifications: 1 year of professional kitchen experience. Graduation from a professional culinary training program may substitute for some experience. High school diploma or GED . High standard of personal hygiene. Valid WA State Food Handler’s Permit or the ability to obtain one before beginning employment. Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background checks. Preferred Qualifications: Working knowledge of computer applications. Valid driver’s license (Must have had a valid driver’s license for at least 2 years). In order to drive campus vehicles, employees must pass PLU’s Driver’s Certification Training. Work Conditions: Work in/around hot, dry, cold, wet, and/or humid conditions. Often work with/around standard kitchen/dishroom equipment requiring protective equipment. Summer schedules vary from the last week in May-August. Physical Requirements: Ability to stand and walk for up to 4 hours without a break. Ability to bend and stoop to grasp objects, climb ladders, lift loads up to 50 lbs. unassisted, and push/pull carts weighing up to 100 lbs. unassisted. Ability to visually examine products for quality and signage/labeling for accuracy. Special Instructions to Applicants: Other Information: PLU is a small, private university where caring means more than kindness and consideration — it means a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a diverse faculty, staff, and student body. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and minoritized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research or other work activities. PLU offers an excellent benefits package including tuition remission for employees and their dependents, retirement plan, medical, dental, paid vacation and sick leave, major holidays off (including Christmas/New Year’s break), additional flexible time off, and many other great university benefits. PLU is a qualifying employer under the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program as a 501©(3) tax-exempt organization. Click here for detailed benefits information. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a valid WA Food Handler's Permit or the ability to obtain one before beginning employment? Yes No * Do you have a High School Diploma or GED? Yes No * Are you 18 years of age or older? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Optional Documents Cover Letter List of Three References Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8933": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor/Instructor of Biology",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Visiting Assistant Professor/Instructor of Biology Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Visiting Assistant Professor/Instructor of Biology Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit the following: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae; List of three references; Statement of teaching philosophy in which you describe your goals for student learning, the methods you use to help students achieve them, and how you assess student learning. This statement should explicitly address how you will create an inclusive learning environment that is attuned to diversity and equity. For questions or more information, please contact Amy Siegesmund at siegesam@plu.edu Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603145 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Visiting Assistant Professor/Instructor of Biology Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Not Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: $8,100 for 1.5 course load. Location Tacoma, WA 98447 Department: Biology Department Posting Date: 09/30/2026 Closing Date Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Work Schedule: Part-time position available for Spring semester only beginning February 1, 2027. General Description: The Biology Department at Pacific Lutheran University is seeking applicants who are committed to excellence in teaching for a part-time position for the Spring 2027 semester only. The successful applicant will teach human anatomy and physiology laboratory (2 sections) for pre-nursing students. The biology department is a vibrant, student-centered academic community committed to helping students understand the complexity and diversity of life while developing the skills to think critically, ask meaningful questions, and engage in scientific discovery. Our faculty represent a broad range of biological disciplines, including molecular and cellular biology, microbiology, anatomy and physiology, developmental biology, ecology, and evolution. We emphasize learning biology by doing biology, with hands-on laboratory and field experiences, undergraduate research, and close faculty-student mentoring woven throughout the curriculum. Our students pursue diverse pathways into health professions, graduate and professional programs, research, environmental science, education, and other careers in the life sciences. We are looking for an individual who identifies first and foremost as an educator and is dedicated to using effective and inclusive teaching methods and practices. Our students come from various backgrounds with different values and beliefs. Many of them are first-generation college students, non-traditional students, students of color, and LGBTQ students. We particularly welcome applications from individuals who can facilitate discussions across diverse perspectives and who share our commitment to promoting diversity, inclusion, and equity in their teaching approaches. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Teach assigned courses at the undergraduate level. Hold regular student hours. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Solid background in human anatomy and physiology. Knowledge of effective and inclusive teaching and learning strategies. Commitment to working effectively with a diverse faculty and student body. Demonstrated enthusiasm for teaching at the undergraduate level within a liberal arts setting. Ability to communicate effectively. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in biology or related discipline must be in hand by February 1, 2027 for appointment at the Visiting Assistant Professor level. ABD or MS in biology or related discipline required by February 1, 2027 for appointment at the Instructor level. Applicant must be legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications: Previous teaching experience at the undergraduate level. Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants should submit the following: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae; List of three references; Statement of teaching philosophy in which you describe your goals for student learning, the methods you use to help students achieve them, and how you assess student learning. This statement should explicitly address how you will create an inclusive learning environment that is attuned to diversity and equity. For questions or more information, please contact Amy Siegesmund at siegesam@plu.edu Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2025-26 academic year, 45 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 51.4 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,800 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Will you have a Master's degree or higher in hand by the time of appointment? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy List of Three References Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8919": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biology - Human Anatomy &amp; Physiology (Revised Salary)",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Assistant Professor of Biology - Human Anatomy &amp; Physiology (Revised Salary) Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Assistant Professor of Biology - Human Anatomy & Physiology (Revised Salary) Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit the following: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae; Research agenda; List of three references; Statement of teaching philosophy in which you describe your goals for student learning, the methods you use to help students achieve them, and how you assess student learning. This statement should explicitly address how you will create an inclusive learning environment that is attuned to diversity and equity. For questions or more information, please contact Amy Siegesmund at siegesam@plu.edu Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603121 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Assistant Professor of Biology - Human Anatomy & Physiology (Revised Salary) Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: $65,000-$75,000 per year + benefits package Location Tacoma, WA Department: Biology Department Posting Date: 09/18/2026 Closing Date Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Work Schedule: Full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position available September 1, 2027. The academic year is from September to May. Classes begin on September 7, 2027. New Faculty Orientation will take place in late August. General Description: The Department of Biology at Pacific Lutheran University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor beginning September 1, 2027. The successful applicant will have an expertise in teaching human anatomy and physiology. The position carries a teaching load of 6 courses per year with both lecture and lab sections counting toward that total. The individual’s annual teaching responsibilities will include participation in the human anatomy and physiology sequence, including the use of cadaveric specimens. In addition, the successful candidate will teach small, engaging upper-division courses in anatomy and/or physiology and will have the opportunity to develop courses aligned with their areas of expertise. We also seek a candidate who will maintain an active scholarship program that complements and broadens the department’s existing strengths while providing opportunities for undergraduate student participation. Scholarship may include disciplinary research, pedagogical research, or other scholarly activities appropriate to the candidate’s area of expertise. Interdisciplinary collaborations with other academic units, including Kinesiology, are welcomed. The biology department is a vibrant, student-centered academic community committed to helping students understand the complexity and diversity of life while developing the skills to think critically, ask meaningful questions, and engage in scientific discovery. Our faculty represent a broad range of biological disciplines, including molecular and cellular biology, microbiology, anatomy and physiology, developmental biology, ecology, and evolution. We emphasize learning biology by doing biology , with hands-on laboratory and field experiences, undergraduate research, and close faculty-student mentoring woven throughout the curriculum. Our students pursue diverse pathways into health professions, graduate and professional programs, research, environmental science, education, and other careers in the life sciences. We are looking for an individual who identifies first and foremost as an educator and is dedicated to using effective and inclusive teaching methods and practices. Our students come from various backgrounds with different values and beliefs. Many of them are first-generation college students, non-traditional students, students of color, and LGBTQ students. We particularly welcome applications from individuals who can facilitate discussions across diverse perspectives and who share our commitment to promoting diversity, inclusion, and equity in their teaching approaches. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Teach assigned courses at the undergraduate level, which includes upper division courses. Engage undergraduates in their scholarship. Hold regular student hours. Attend regular department meetings. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Strong background in anatomy and physiology. Knowledge of effective and inclusive teaching and learning strategies. Commitment to working effectively with a diverse faculty and student body. Demonstrated enthusiasm for teaching at the undergraduate level within a liberal arts setting. Ability to communicate effectively. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in biology or related field. ABD will be considered, but Ph.D. must be in hand by September 1, 2027. The applicant must be legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications: Experience working with cadavers. Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants should submit the following: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae; Research agenda; List of three references; Statement of teaching philosophy in which you describe your goals for student learning, the methods you use to help students achieve them, and how you assess student learning. This statement should explicitly address how you will create an inclusive learning environment that is attuned to diversity and equity. For questions or more information, please contact Amy Siegesmund at siegesam@plu.edu Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2025-26 academic year, 45 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 51.4 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,800 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Click here for detailed benefits information. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Will you have a PhD or equivalent in hand by the date of appointment? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy List of Three References Research Agenda Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8950": {
      "title": "IT Support Specialist",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | IT Support Specialist Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home IT Support Specialist Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603125 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: IT Support Specialist Position Type: Staff Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Non-Exempt Hiring Range: $20.75-$22.31 per hour, plus excellent benefits. Location Tacoma, WA 98447. Department: User Support Posting Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date When a sufficient number of qualified applicants have been identified. Work Schedule: Monday-Friday, 8:00am to 5:00pm; occasional evening or weekend support for technology issues may be requested. General Description: The IT Support Specialist provides comprehensive hardware and software support for faculty, staff, and students at PLU . Core duties include the installation, maintenance, and upgrading of campus-wide technology in labs, classrooms, and offices, as well as resolving campus copier and printing issues. This role reports to the User Support Team Manager. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Diagnose and solve problems with university-owned Windows and Apple computer equipment, including devices used in classrooms, labs and meeting spaces. Work with the User Support team to provide general user support for help desk tickets as needed. Support and maintain the campus printers/copiers, including driver installation, network configuration management, and coordinating repairs. Manage classrooms and lab computers, providing both hardware and software support when necessary. This includes providing updated asset information to appropriate staff in order to maintain hardware and software inventory as needed. Coordinate and provide both security and software patches (preventative maintenance) on all classrooms and labs as needed. Diagnose, research, and resolve installation and implementation problems with all supported software or equipment. Develop, maintain, and improve training materials, handouts and reference guides. Attend User Support weekly meetings and discuss ongoing issues/projects. Perform other user support duties as assigned by User Support Team Manager. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Strong customer service skills. Excellent communication (written and oral), organization, and conflict-resolution skills. Ability to remain calm during times of increased activity and stress. Ability to prioritize assigned tasks, use critical thinking and problem solving skills. Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Ability to work both independently and with a team. Ability to quickly learn and problem solve in a constantly changing software environment. Ability to diagnose and resolve problems utilizing technical resources available on web and in print. Familiarity with operating systems including Windows and MacOS. Basic understanding of computer networks and related hardware and software. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree OR two years job related experience in software support on both Windows and Apple computers in a networked environment. Education may substitute for some experience. High school diploma or GED . Final applicants must satisfactorily complete a pre-employment background check. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree with coursework in computer science or business. Experience with working with a help desk ticket tracking system. Experience working in Windows Active Directory environment. Experience working in Jamf & PDQ mobile device management environment. Relevant software and OS certifications for working with Microsoft Windows and Apple products. Experience in higher education supporting students, faculty, and staff. Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Moving computer equipment w/cart (up to 50 lbs) when needed. Special Instructions to Applicants: Other Information: PLU is a small, private university where caring means more than kindness and consideration — it means a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a diverse faculty, staff, and student body. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and minoritized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research or other work activities. PLU offers an excellent benefits package including tuition remission for employees and their dependents, retirement plan, medical, dental, paid vacation and sick leave, major holidays off (including Christmas/New Year’s break), additional flexible time off, and many other great university benefits. PLU is a qualifying employer under the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program as a 501©(3) tax-exempt organization. Click here for detailed benefits information. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a High School Diploma or GED? Yes No * Are you 18 years of age or older? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Resume Optional Documents List of Three References Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8957": {
      "title": "Open Rank Professor and Director of the M.S. in Cybersecurity",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Open Rank Professor and Director of the M.S. in Cybersecurity Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Open Rank Professor and Director of the M.S. in Cybersecurity Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. To apply, please submit your application including: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae; A statement of teaching philosophy; List of three references with contact information; List of courses prepared to teach; Tenure-track candidates should also submit a research agenda, which may be uploaded under “Optional Documents.” For questions or more information, please contact Laurie Murphy, Chair of Computer Science, at lmurphy@plu.edu . Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603152 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Open Rank Professor and Director of the M.S. in Cybersecurity Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: $75,000 to $140,000 per year + benefits package. Location Tacoma, WA 98447 Department: Computer Science Department Posting Date: 10/27/2026 Closing Date Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Work Schedule: Full-time, 9 month tenure-track or non-tenure-track position available beginning Spring or Summer 2027. Teach 4 classes during the first academic year, and 3 courses in subsequent years, depending on the needs of the program. Administrative responsibilities: Director of the M.S. in Cybersecurity program. The academic year is from September to May. General Description: The Department of Computer Science at Pacific Lutheran University seeks a Program Director who will lead and teach in our new M.S. degree in Cybersecurity, with the position beginning in Spring or Summer 2027. This position may be tenure-track (hiring at the level of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor, based upon qualifications) or offered as a renewable fixed-term contract (visiting or resident). We are seeking a passionate, driven, motivated leader to take a pivotal role in shaping this program, including designing courses and aiding in recruiting students to launch the program in Fall 2027. This is an exciting opportunity to lead the design, implementation and continuous improvement of the program, ensuring alignment with industry standards and emerging trends. The selected applicant will join and lead an exciting new graduate degree, building upon PLU’s existing Computer Science baccalaureate program. Applicants should be able to assume the responsibilities of teaching graduate-level courses in the program, further refining the curriculum, hiring faculty, marketing the program, and recruiting students. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Serve as Director for the M.S. in Cybersecurity program: design courses for the program; recruit and support faculty and students; manage the day-to-day operations in the program including budgeting, scheduling and resource allocation. Develop and teach 4 classes during the first academic year (September 2027 – May 2028), and teach 3 classes in subsequent years, depending on the needs of the program. Lead the design, implementation, and continuous improvement of the MS in Cybersecurity curriculum, ensuring alignment with industry standards and emerging trends. Serve in the Computer Science Department and contribute to the larger life of the University through advising, committee work, and other service activities. Provide inspiring and visionary leadership to advance the M.S. in Cybersecurity program at PLU . Communicate effectively; collaborate effectively and constructively with colleagues within the Computer Science Department and across disciplines. Be committed to graduate teaching in a university that blends undergraduate liberal arts and graduate professional education. Have a clearly defined scholarship agenda relevant to cybersecurity (for tenure-track candidates). Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Strong background in computer science and cybersecurity. Demonstrated commitment to inclusive and anti-racist pedagogical practices. Demonstrated enthusiasm for teaching at the graduate level within a liberal arts setting. Ability to communicate and collaborate effectively with others. Strong understanding of current cybersecurity challenges and trends. Required Qualifications: PhD in Cybersecurity, Computer Science, Information Technology or related field. Evidence of teaching excellence and a demonstrated concern for improving the quality of their teaching. Demonstrated competence in scholarship, along with the potential for continued scholarly achievement (for tenure-track candidates). Applicant must be legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications: Previous teaching experience at the graduate level in cybersecurity. Previous leadership experience in a university or other professional setting. Active involvement in professional organizations related to cybersecurity. Experience in online or hybrid course delivery. Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: To apply, please submit your application including: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae; A statement of teaching philosophy; List of three references with contact information; List of courses prepared to teach; Tenure-track candidates should also submit a research agenda, which may be uploaded under “Optional Documents.” For questions or more information, please contact Laurie Murphy, Chair of Computer Science, at lmurphy@plu.edu . Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2025-26 academic year, 45 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 51.4 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,700 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty- shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Oﬃce at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Click here for detailed benefits information. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Will you have a doctorate degree in hand by the date of appointment? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now or indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae List of Three References Teaching Philosophy List of Courses Prepared to Teach Optional Documents Graduate Transcript Teaching Evaluations Research Agenda Undergraduate Transcript Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8951": {
      "title": "Professional Safety Officer (Multiple Positions Available)",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Professional Safety Officer (Multiple Positions Available) Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Professional Safety Officer (Multiple Positions Available) Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603142 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Professional Safety Officer (Multiple Positions Available) Position Type: Staff Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Non-Exempt Hiring Range: $22.36 per hour with excellent benefits. Location Tacoma, WA 98447. Department: Campus Safety & Information Posting Date: 10/06/2026 Closing Date When a sufficient number of qualified applicants have been identified. Work Schedule: Full-time, available shifts are: Thursday – Sunday: 6:00am-4:00pm Friday – Monday: 8:00pm-6:00am Shift times and dates may be adjusted for training or department needs. This position is eligible to receive a pay differential of $1.00 per hour, for hours worked between 9:00pm and 4:59am. General Description: The Professional Safety Officer is responsible for the safety and security of the Pacific Lutheran University campus and its personnel. This is accomplished through providing a presence on campus, interacting positively and professionally with community members and guests, and performing a variety of daily tasks. Reports to the Assistant Director of Campus Safety Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Engage with community members in a culturally responsive manner and with the goal of developing on-going relationships. Proactively monitor activity on campus by providing presence on campus on foot or in a vehicle. Lock and unlock buildings and perimeter gates and check fire panels in buildings. Assist individuals in locating or obtaining resources to address issues or special needs. Respond to emergency incidents, ensuring proper procedures are followed and notifications are made. Administer first aid and operate an AED machine. Assist local fire and law enforcement personnel in the performance of their duties as requested. Provide assistance with vehicle jump starts, walking escorts, and shuttle transports. Use weaponless defensive tactics, employing verbal skills to diffuse volatile or hostile situations. Perform dispatcher duties including, answering multi-line phones, entering data into the computer system, and addressing walk-in visitors. Write reports on incidents. Operate university vehicles in conformance with local laws and PLU policies. Attend professional development training such as Security Officer Academy and ICS 100/200 and 700 certifications. Train new employees on department policies and procedures. Perform other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Excellent human relation skills; ability to deal with a wide variety of people; intercultural skill and capacity necessary to effectively engage individuals from a range of salient identities. Knowledge and skill in confrontation management. Ability to follow department policies, standard operating procedures, and directives. Ability to multi-task and make decisions quickly. Knowledge of radio operations, video console operations, and driving operations. Ability to deal with a variety of personalities in stressful situations. Ability to effectively and clearly read, write, speak and communicate the English language. Ability to communicate clearly on a handheld radio. Knowledge of basic computer skills such as MS Word, MS Excel, Google Mail, and Calendar, and use of the internet. Required Qualifications: 2 years of job-related safety/security experience, customer service experience, or equivalent training. High school diploma or GED . Valid driver’s license (Must have had a valid driver’s license for at least 2 years). First aid/ CPR / AED certification required and provided upon employment. Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background checks, including a driving abstract check, and pass PLU’s Driver’s Certification Training. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. Experience in an academic setting. Knowledge of PLU and its campus. Experience within a safety or security setting. Experience writing activity reports on a regular basis Work Conditions: Work is normally split between indoor office work and the field patrol environment. Exposure to all kinds of weather conditions and occasional exposure to physical risk. Physical Requirements: Ability to walk, run, or climb stairs for extended periods of time. Ability to sit and operate computer related equipment for extended periods of time. Special Instructions to Applicants: Other Information: PLU is a small, private university where caring means more than kindness and consideration — it means a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a diverse faculty, staff, and student body. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and minoritized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research or other work activities. PLU offers an excellent benefits package including tuition remission for employees and their dependents, generous retirement plan, medical, dental, paid vacation and sick leave, major holidays off (including Christmas/New Year’s break and extra time off during the summer), and many other great university benefits. PLU is a qualifying employer under the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program as a 501©(3) tax-exempt organization. Click here for detailed benefits information. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you 18 years of age or older? Yes No * Do you have a current valid driver's license which you have had for at least 2 years? Yes No * Do you have a High School Diploma or GED? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Resume List of Three References Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8958": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor and DSW Program Director",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Assistant/Associate/Full Professor and DSW Program Director Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Assistant/Associate/Full Professor and DSW Program Director Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit the following: Cover letter that includes an overview of your experience as a clinical social worker, social work program leader, and social work faculty member coordinating program accreditation or reaccreditation. Your letter should address how these experiences have prepared you to lead a program focused on the preparation of DSW students. Curriculum Vitae List of Three Professional References Statement on your teaching and/or training experiences that includes: 1) the pedagogical methods that support students from diverse backgrounds, including your attention to the diverse perspectives or methodologies regarding the material you assign; and 2) a description of how, through mentoring and/or community service, you would create an inclusive learning environment in spaces beyond the classroom that support diversity, equity, and anti-racism (maximum 1,000 words). For questions or more information, please contact Ren Winnett, Search Committee Chair, at rwinnett@plu.edu Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603147 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor and DSW Program Director Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: Salary commensurate with experience and rank + benefits package. Assistant: $65,000 - $70,000/yr; Associate: $70,000 - $85,000/yr; Full: $80,000 - $87,500/yr. Location Tacoma, WA 98447 Department: Social Work Department Posting Date: 10/30/2026 Closing Date Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Work Schedule: Full-time, 9-month, tenure-track position available September 1, 2027. The academic year is from September to May. Classes begin on September 7, 2027. New Faculty Orientation will take place on September 1, 2027. General Description: The Pacific Lutheran University Department of Social Work invites applications for a full-time tenure-track position to start in Fall 2027. Applicants should be able to teach generalist foundation and practice courses, as well as advanced course content involving practice, theory, and research. Applicants should have experience teaching at the BSW , MSW , and, ideally, doctoral level (either DSW or Ph.D.). Position responsibilities will involve teaching three courses per academic year, developing a DSW program, pursuing CSWE accreditation for the DSW program, and serving as DSW program chair. Diversity is a core value and foundational concept in the department, and we are committed to building and supporting an inclusive and diverse faculty, staff, and student community. Almost half of our current and recent graduating cohorts have been made up of students of color. Additionally, we have strong ties to the local community and place a strong emphasis on collaborative approaches to complex social issues. Our faculty seek to advance PLU’s vision of academic distinction through our emphasis on helping students find their own paths to meaningful and successful lives, engaging in student-faculty research and service projects, and serving the university, the department, and the community, and by. The BSW and MSW programs are accredited by the Council on Social Work Education; the BSW and has maintained accreditation since 1975. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Oversee tasks involving DSW program development, CSWE accreditation, staff recruitment, student admission, oversight, and evaluation. Teach 3 courses per year, including some combination of BSW , MSW , and/or DSW social work micro/mezzo practice courses, field seminar, introductory social work classes ( HBSE ), and elective(s) or interdisciplinary programs such as First Year Experience ( FYEP ) or International Honors ( IHON ), and study away opportunities. Serve in the department and contribute to the larger life of PLU through advising, committee work, and other service activities. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: We seek collegial faculty who view themselves foremost as educational leaders and possess a commitment to, and knowledge of, effective teaching strategies and program leadership. Familiarity with and commitment to teaching generalist and advanced practice social work courses. Commitment to and background working with, teaching, or mentoring diverse groups and/or diverse students. Ability to contribute to fostering diversity of the campus, the curriculum, and/or academic division. Commitment to undergraduate and graduate teaching in a program that blends the liberal arts with a generalist-focused professional education in social work. Knowledge of CSWE accrediting standards. Interest in developing a DSW program and managing the CSWE accrediting process. Required Qualifications: Doctorate in Social Work. ABD will be considered, but doctorate degree must be in hand by the start date of the position. MSW from a CSWE accredited program plus two years-post MSW practice experience. Clearly defined scholarship agenda relevant to social work as well as a commitment to effective teaching and program leadership. The applicant must be legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date and legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications: Experience serving individuals, families, groups, and/or communities as a social worker. Teaching experience at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Teaching experience at the doctoral level. Established record of scholarship. Experience with social work program development and leadership. Experience with social work program accreditation and/or reaccreditation. Current clinical license, or license eligible within 1 year. Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants should submit the following: Cover letter that includes an overview of your experience as a clinical social worker, social work program leader, and social work faculty member coordinating program accreditation or reaccreditation. Your letter should address how these experiences have prepared you to lead a program focused on the preparation of DSW students. Curriculum Vitae List of Three Professional References Statement on your teaching and/or training experiences that includes: 1) the pedagogical methods that support students from diverse backgrounds, including your attention to the diverse perspectives or methodologies regarding the material you assign; and 2) a description of how, through mentoring and/or community service, you would create an inclusive learning environment in spaces beyond the classroom that support diversity, equity, and anti-racism (maximum 1,000 words). For questions or more information, please contact Ren Winnett, Search Committee Chair, at rwinnett@plu.edu Other Information: The Department of Social Work has approximately 80 declared social work majors and several undergraduate students seeking to minor in social work, as well as a total of approximately 60 graduate students in advanced standing and regular standing MSW cohorts. The Department is well known for the development of generalist BSW practice social workers, who, upon completion of their degree, are ready to engage in advocacy, case management, community development, and in the child welfare system, with seniors, in childcare settings, in youth organizations, in corrections, in mental health, and with people with disabilities to improve the lives of individuals from marginalized or oppressed groups. The MSW program is entering its fourth year of operation with a focus on health and behavioral health and is experiencing steeply growing demand. The DSW program is currently in its initial development stage. Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2025-26 academic year, 45 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 51.4 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,800 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Click here for detailed benefits information. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Will you have a doctorate degree in hand by the date of appointment? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae List of Three References Statement of Teaching Strategies Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    }
  },
